<div class="overlay-panel" id="governor-welcome" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="governor-welcome-title" aria-hidden="true">
  <div class="overlay-card overlay-card--letter" tabindex="-1">
    <a href="#" class="overlay-close" data-modal-close aria-label="Close the governor’s welcome">Close</a>
    <p class="overlay-eyebrow">Office of Governor Towles</p>
    <h2 id="governor-welcome-title">Welcome to Maia IV</h2>
    <div class="governor-letter">
      <p>Welcome.</p>
      <p>Whether you have crossed the quadrant for treatment, research, work, or simple curiosity, we are grateful that your journey has brought you here.</p>
      <p>The Federation is home to nearly one trillion citizens. Maia IV is home to just 243,000. We are a small community entrusted with an ancient world.</p>
      <p>The civilization that came before us left no living voice, but its evidence remains—in inscriptions, foundations, mathematics, and observations of the natural world. Much of what appears modern here began with something they left behind: a pattern recognized, a principle recovered, or a question we are still learning how to ask.</p>
      <p>We do not consider ourselves the owners of that legacy. We are its stewards. This belief shapes how we build, how we practice medicine, and how we live beside a history far older than our own.</p>
      <p>I first came to Maia IV from Alpha Centauri to serve in medicine. I expected my work here to be temporary. Instead, this world became my home, medicine led me into civic service, and its people entrusted me with the privilege of serving as their governor.</p>
      <p>While you are here, we ask that you share in our stewardship. Please review the current travel advisories before leaving the arrival district. Protected landscapes beyond the marked corridors remain restricted for your safety and for the preservation of sites still under study.</p>
      <p>Most visitors arrive knowing precisely when they intend to leave. Maia IV has a way of making that date feel provisional.</p>
      <p>On behalf of my office and the 243,000 citizens who call this world home: welcome to Maia IV.</p>
      <div class="governor-signature">
        <img class="governor-signature-mark" src="/images/savannah-towles.svg" alt="Savannah Towles" />
        <span class="governor-triad-symbol" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <p>Governor · Maia IV Administration<br /><span>By authority of the Queen’s Triad</span><br /><span class="governor-stardate">Stardate: 79881.279</span></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="overlay-panel" id="first-survey-log" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="first-survey-log-title" aria-hidden="true">
  <div class="overlay-card overlay-card--archive" tabindex="-1">
    <a href="#" class="overlay-close" data-modal-close aria-label="Close the first survey log">Close</a>
    <p class="overlay-eyebrow">Starfleet Archive · Initial Planetary Contact and Survey</p>
    <h2 id="first-survey-log-title">The Maia IV Logs</h2>
    <dl class="log-record-meta" aria-label="Archive record details">
      <div><dt>Vessel</dt><dd>USS Enterprise · NCC-1701-D</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Commanding officer</dt><dd>Captain Jean-Luc Picard</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Initial entry</dt><dd>Stardate 41341.4</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Record status</dt><dd>Declassified · Complete</dd></div>
    </dl>

    <div class="captains-log">
      <article class="log-entry">
        <header>
          <p>Captain’s Log</p>
          <h3>Stardate 41341.4</h3>
        </header>
        <p>Following the events at Farpoint Station—and our unsettling encounter with the entity identifying himself as Q—the Enterprise has resumed its assigned mission of exploration. Our recent mission to Mordan IV was a sobering reminder that decisions made in the name of expediency can shape a civilization for generations. A conventional planetary survey has therefore been a welcome change of pace for the crew.</p>
        <p>The Enterprise entered orbit of the fourth planet in the Maia system this morning. Our initial survey identified a temperate Class-M world with extensive oceans, a breathable atmosphere, and a remarkably diverse biosphere.</p>
        <p>It appears, however, that our assignment will not remain conventional for long.</p>
        <p>Away teams are already discovering evidence that Maia IV was once inhabited by an advanced civilization.</p>
        <p>The planet is extraordinarily beautiful. Its daylight sky is a deep tangerine, crossed by clouds of pale celadon. Doctor Crusher and Lieutenant Commander Data have determined that immense colonies of photosynthetic aeroplankton inhabit the upper atmosphere and cloud-forming layers. Their pigments alter the passage of light, reducing the system’s stellar bodies to broad, diffused forms during daylight.</p>
        <p>After sunset, the aeroplankton descend into the denser atmosphere. The tangerine color recedes, revealing the darkest cobalt sky I have ever personally witnessed. Three natural satellites become visible above the horizon—a nocturnal counterpart to the three lights that govern the day.</p>
        <p>The ruins discovered thus far are extensive, but their age and purpose remain unknown. Some stand openly upon the landscape. Others lie beneath forests composed of fungal organisms of extraordinary size. There are roads without destinations, foundations without buildings, and monuments bearing inscriptions our computers have not yet translated.</p>
        <p>Little more can be said with certainty. We are gathering samples, recording the locations of each site, and cataloguing what we have found.</p>
        <p>For the moment, Maia IV offers us something increasingly rare: a question untouched by an answer.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="log-entry">
        <header>
          <p>Captain’s Log</p>
          <h3>Supplemental</h3>
        </header>
        <p>Commander Riker’s team has confirmed that the ruins are not confined to a single settlement. Orbital scans have identified artificial structures on five continents, with additional formations beneath the shallow seas.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant Yar’s tactical analysis has found no evidence of planetary bombardment, sustained weapons fire, or a coordinated assault from orbit. Several sites include structures that may once have served a defensive purpose, but the damage we have observed thus far is consistent with age, geological movement, and prolonged exposure—not warfare. Whatever ended this civilization, it does not appear to have been a conventional military catastrophe.</p>
        <p>Commander Data has discovered a recurring mathematical system within the inscriptions. Measurements, architectural proportions, and astronomical diagrams are all expressed in powers of three. Even apparently decorative elements resolve into repeating triadic sequences when examined together.</p>
        <p>The civilization that constructed these sites possessed a sophisticated understanding of astronomy. Several monuments are aligned not merely with the movement of the system’s three stars, but with the orbital positions of Maia IV’s three moons. The precision of those alignments suggests observations conducted over many thousands of years.</p>
        <p>Two related configurations appear repeatedly. One depicts three stellar figures arranged around a common center. The other follows the three satellites through a sequence that our translation programs render provisionally as emergence, measure, and ending.</p>
        <p>The closest analogues in Earth mythology would be queens and the three Fates. I have used those terms in the preliminary catalogue, but they remain Federation Standard approximations of ideas that originated here.</p>
        <p>We have found no evidence in the surveyed sites of warfare, planetary catastrophe, or mass evacuation. We have also found no burial grounds, no preserved remains, and no indication of where the inhabitants went.</p>
        <p>A civilization once ordered its understanding of the universe around this world.</p>
        <p>Then, at some point in the distant past, it disappeared.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="log-entry">
        <header>
          <p>Captain’s Log</p>
          <h3>Supplemental</h3>
        </header>
        <p>Our biological survey has revealed that the relationship between the ruins and the surrounding environment is considerably more complex than we first believed.</p>
        <p>Doctor Crusher has identified unusual genetic regularities within several species growing near the archaeological sites. The enormous fungal forests are connected by subterranean mycelial networks extending for hundreds of kilometers. Whether these organisms were cultivated, altered, or simply protected by the planet’s inhabitants remains unclear.</p>
        <p>The atmospheric aeroplankton may also have been the subject of deliberate stewardship. Their daily migration regulates the amount and character of stellar light reaching the surface and contributes to the planet’s unusually stable climate. If this relationship was encouraged by the ancient inhabitants, their influence remains present in every sunrise and every breath of air.</p>
        <p>Commander Data has completed a comparison of the major ruins. What initially appeared to be separate cities may instead be components of a planetary arrangement. Their positions correspond to relationships among the three stars and three moons.</p>
        <p>The pattern appears throughout the surviving record: in mathematics, architecture, astronomy, and what may have been systems of civic or symbolic thought.</p>
        <p>The names in our catalogue are ours. The pattern is not.</p>
        <p>This distinction must remain clear. We did not bring meaning to Maia IV. We have merely begun to recognize meaning that was already here.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="log-entry">
        <header>
          <p>Captain’s Log</p>
          <h3>Supplemental</h3>
        </header>
        <p>Our survey of the Maia system has revealed that the archaeological significance of its fourth planet is not the only matter likely to attract Federation attention.</p>
        <p>The outer planets possess considerable strategic value. Three gas giants contain substantial concentrations of deuterium and helium-3, while several of their moons exhibit dilithium-bearing geological formations. Long-range scans have also identified extensive deposits of duranium, tritanium, and other industrial metals throughout the outer asteroid fields.</p>
        <p>The system occupies a favorable position near the convergence of several projected transit routes through this region of the Alpha Quadrant. With the appropriate infrastructure, Maia could support scientific exploration, civilian traffic, and Starfleet operations beyond the present survey boundary.</p>
        <p>Ordinarily, these findings would make the establishment of a permanent Federation presence here an uncomplicated recommendation.</p>
        <p>Maia IV makes the matter considerably more difficult.</p>
        <p>The resources of the outer system can be approached with care and used without disturbing the planet below. But their value will inevitably bring ships, personnel, commerce, and pressure for settlement. Any protection afforded to the archaeological sites must therefore be established before that development begins—not after damage has already been done.</p>
        <p>The question is not whether the Federation will return to Maia.</p>
        <p>It is what we will choose to preserve when we do.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="log-entry log-entry--final">
        <header>
          <p>Captain’s Log</p>
          <h3>Final</h3>
        </header>
        <p>The Enterprise has completed its initial survey of Maia IV.</p>
        <p>We have been ordered to Starbase 74, where the Enterprise will undergo routine maintenance and upgrades to several computer systems. Our departure leaves many questions unanswered, but the record we carry with us should ensure that Maia IV will not remain unknown for long.</p>
        <p>The planet is ideally suited for Federation settlement. Its atmosphere is breathable, its climate temperate, and its native gravity approximately eighty percent of Earth standard. Its oceans, fertile regions, and stable stellar environment could comfortably support a substantial population.</p>
        <p>Those same qualities place Maia IV in immediate danger.</p>
        <p>Without protection, the planet would attract settlement, development, private expeditions, and the unauthorized removal of cultural material. Even responsible activity could contaminate sites that have remained undisturbed for millennia. Roads, agriculture, and habitation could destroy relationships between the ruins and the living environment before we understand their significance.</p>
        <p>I will therefore recommend through Starfleet Command that the Federation Council designate Maia IV a planetary preserve.</p>
        <p>Limited scientific access should be permitted under strict supervision. A research installation may eventually be necessary, both to support continued study and to protect the archaeological record. Permanent settlement may also prove appropriate, but construction should proceed only within areas that have been thoroughly surveyed and cleared. Until the extent of this civilization is understood, expansion beyond those areas would represent an unacceptable risk.</p>
        <p>We should not mistake the absence of a living population for the absence of a claim upon this world.</p>
        <p>The people of Maia IV left no voice we can hear and no name by which we may call them. But they remain present—in their mathematics, in their monuments, in the organisms they may have cultivated, and in a pattern that encompasses the entire planet.</p>
        <p>We came here believing we were the first to chart Maia IV.</p>
        <p>We were mistaken.</p>
        <p>Our task now is not to claim what we have found, but to ensure that what has endured here is not lost through our arrival.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <footer class="log-archive-note">
      <span>Historical note</span>
      <p>The Enterprise was the first Federation starship to survey Maia IV by circumstance rather than special assignment. The record is preserved for its observations and recommendations—not as a claim of discovery or ownership.</p>
    </footer>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="overlay-panel" id="preservation-charter" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="preservation-charter-title" aria-hidden="true">
  <div class="overlay-card overlay-card--archive" tabindex="-1">
    <a href="#" class="overlay-close" data-modal-close aria-label="Close the preservation and settlement charter">Close</a>
    <p class="overlay-eyebrow">Federation Council Archive</p>
    <h2 id="preservation-charter-title" class="archive-document-title">Maia IV Preservation and Settlement Charter</h2>

    <dl class="log-record-meta council-record-meta" aria-label="Council record details">
      <div><dt>Instrument</dt><dd>Federation Council Resolution</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Adopted</dt><dd>Stardate 42259.2</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Status</dt><dd>In force · Subsequently amended</dd></div>
    </dl>

    <section class="council-record">
      <div class="council-record__body">
        <p class="council-record__preamble">Having reviewed Starfleet Initial Survey Record 41341, the recommendations submitted by Captain Jean-Luc Picard, and the subsequent findings of the Federation Science Council concerning the fourth planet of the Maia system, the Federation Council hereby resolves:</p>

        <ol class="charter-articles">
          <li>
            <h4>Planetary designation</h4>
            <p>Maia IV is designated a Federation planetary preserve and heritage world. This designation recognizes the planet’s archaeological record, indigenous biosphere, and surviving cultural landscapes as resources held in trust for continued study.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h4>Presumption of protection</h4>
            <p>All known archaeological sites, untranslated inscriptions, subsurface repositories, associated ecological systems, and territories not yet surveyed shall be presumed protected. Absence of recorded evidence shall not constitute clearance for development.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h4>Maia Station</h4>
            <p>A permanent administrative, scientific, and civilian settlement is authorized within the initial development zone. The settlement shall be designated Maia Station and shall serve as the principal point of access to Maia IV and the wider system.</p>
            <p>No fixed population limit is established by this charter. Expansion may proceed within surveyed and cleared territory. Enlargement of the development zone requires archaeological, ecological, and subsurface review.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h4>Cultural material</h4>
            <p>Artifacts and records originating on Maia IV shall remain under Federation heritage protection. Removal from the planet is prohibited except when necessary for preservation or authorized research, and all such material shall remain part of the common Maian record.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h4>Outer-system development</h4>
            <p>Scientific, navigational, and resource operations elsewhere in the Maia system may proceed under Federation authority. Such activity shall be administered independently from the Maia IV preserve and shall not establish an automatic claim to planetary land, access, or development rights.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h4>Maia X orbital station</h4>
            <p>A permanent Federation station is authorized in orbit of Maia X to coordinate navigation, resource operations, scientific missions, freight transfer, and Starfleet logistics throughout the outer system. Heavy industrial and transit activity should be concentrated at the Maia X facility wherever practicable to limit operational pressure on Maia IV.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h4>Administration and review</h4>
            <p>The Federation Bureau of Planetary Heritage shall maintain the protected-site register and advise the Maia IV Administration on proposed development. Preserve boundaries, research corridors, and settlement zones shall remain subject to revision as new evidence is discovered.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>

        <p class="council-record__adoption">Adopted by the Federation Council and entered into the Federation record on Stardate 42259.2.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <footer class="log-archive-note">
      <span>Historical note</span>
      <p>The Council charter adopted Picard’s central preservation argument but did not enact his recommendation unchanged: it authorized permanent settlement and outer-system development alongside the preserve. The present protected landscapes, research corridors, and development zones reflect later surveys, amendments, and successive decisions about where growth could proceed.</p>
    </footer>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="overlay-panel" id="custodial-authority-order" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="custodial-authority-title" aria-hidden="true">
  <div class="overlay-card overlay-card--archive" tabindex="-1">
    <a href="#" class="overlay-close" data-modal-close aria-label="Close the custodial authority transfer">Close</a>
    <p class="overlay-eyebrow">Federation Bureau of Planetary Heritage Archive</p>
    <h2 id="custodial-authority-title" class="archive-document-title">Maia IV Custodial Authority Order</h2>

    <dl class="log-record-meta council-record-meta" aria-label="Custodial authority record details">
      <div><dt>Instrument</dt><dd>Bureau Administrative Order</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Effective</dt><dd>Stardate 43218.6</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Status</dt><dd>Active · Authority subsequently expanded</dd></div>
    </dl>

    <section class="council-record">
      <div class="council-record__body">
        <p class="council-record__preamble">Pursuant to the Maia IV Preservation and Settlement Charter, and following completion of the preliminary planetary site register, the Federation Bureau of Planetary Heritage accepts permanent custodial jurisdiction over the Maia IV preserve.</p>

        <ol class="charter-articles">
          <li>
            <h4>Custodial jurisdiction</h4>
            <p>The Bureau shall serve as the governing authority for protected landscapes, archaeological sites, research preserves, and all territory outside the development zones established for Maia Station. This jurisdiction includes the power to restrict access, suspend activity, and revise protected boundaries when new evidence is discovered.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h4>Stewardship, not ownership</h4>
            <p>Custodial authority shall not be construed as Federation ownership of the extinct Maian civilization, its cultural record, or the planet held in its name. The Bureau receives responsibility for their protection, not title to them. All surviving material shall remain part of the common Maian record and be administered in trust.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h4>Presumption of protection</h4>
            <p>Unsurveyed territory shall remain protected by default. The absence of a catalogued structure, artifact, or biological association shall not constitute permission for settlement or development. Clearance may be granted only after archaeological, ecological, atmospheric, and subsurface review.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h4>Division of authority</h4>
            <p>The Maia IV Administration shall govern Maia Station, its civic institutions, and approved development zones. The Bureau shall govern the planetary preserve and maintain final authority over heritage protection. Decisions affecting both jurisdictions shall be reviewed jointly; neither authority may extend its boundaries unilaterally.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h4>Research and public access</h4>
            <p>The Bureau shall license archaeological and xenoanthropological work, maintain the protected-site register, authorize field stations, and establish supervised research and transit corridors. Public access may be permitted where it does not compromise intact sites, living systems, or ongoing study.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h4>Permanent presence</h4>
            <p>A Bureau directorate shall be established at Maia Station, supported by regional field offices and an independent planetary survey service. Its records shall remain available to the Maia IV Administration, the Federation Science Council, and accredited research institutions.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>

        <p class="council-record__adoption">Authority accepted by the Federation Bureau of Planetary Heritage and entered into the Maia IV administrative record on Stardate 43218.6.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <footer class="log-archive-note">
      <span>Historical note</span>
      <p>This order transformed preservation from Council policy into a permanent system of government. The Bureau did not become the owner of Maia IV: it became custodian of the protected world, while the Maia IV Administration retained civic authority within Maia Station and its approved development zones.</p>
    </footer>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="overlay-panel" id="about-the-project" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="about-title" aria-hidden="true">
